#   - discovery.seed_hosts (other indexer nodes)
#   - cluster.initial_master_nodes

# Generate and distribute TLS certificates for node-to-node and REST API encryption
3. Manager cluster (master + worker)

On the master node, /var/ossec/etc/ossec.conf cluster block:

xml
<cluster>
  <name>wazuh-lab-cluster</name>
  <node_name>master-node</node_name>
  <node_type>master</node_type>
  <key>REPLACE_WITH_GENERATED_CLUSTER_KEY</key>
  <port>1516</port>
  <bind_addr>0.0.0.0</bind_addr>
  <nodes>
    <node>MASTER_NODE_IP</node>
  </nodes>
  <hidden>no</hidden>
  <disabled>no</disabled>
</cluster>

On the worker node, same block but node_type = worker, same cluster key, and <nodes> pointing to the master's IP.

Generate the cluster key with:

bash
openssl rand -hex 16

Verify cluster status:

bash
/var/ossec/bin/cluster_control -l

4. Dashboard
Connected to the indexer cluster via opensearch_dashboards.yml
TLS between dashboard and indexer configured using the same cert set
Verified login and cluster health visible under Server Management → Cluster

5. Agent enrollment
bash
# On each agent (host-laptop, win-server)
/var/ossec/bin/agent-auth -m MANAGER_IP
systemctl restart wazuh-agent
Verified agents appear as Active under Agents in the dashboard
Confirmed events flowing from both Windows agents before moving to rule customization
Confirmed which manager node agents actually report to (wazuh-worker) before debugging "missing" events

6. Validation checklist
 Both manager nodes show in cluster_control -l
 Indexer cluster health is green
 Agents report Active status
 Test alert (e.g., manual file change) appears in dashboard within expected latency
 Services enabled with systemctl enable so they survive a reboot
